---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import LanguageSelector from '../components/LanguageSelector.astro';

const guides = [
	{ id: 'calibrate-screen', category: 'CALIBRATION', number: '01', title: 'How to calibrate an online ruler for your screen', summary: 'Set a useful starting scale, then check it against a physical reference for a closer match.', read: '4 min read' },
	{ id: 'measure-cm-mm', category: 'MEASUREMENT BASICS', number: '02', title: 'Centimeters, millimeters, and inches: a quick guide', summary: 'Read the small marks on a ruler and convert common metric and imperial lengths.', read: '3 min read' },
	{ id: 'phone-ruler', category: 'ON YOUR DEVICE', number: '03', title: 'How to use a screen ruler on a phone or tablet', summary: 'Make the most of a compact screen, choose an edge, and keep the scale easy to read.', read: '3 min read' },
	{ id: 'screen-accuracy', category: 'ACCURACY', number: '04', title: 'How accurate is an online ruler?', summary: 'Understand what affects screen measurements and when to verify with a physical tool.', read: '4 min read' },
];
---

<Layout
	title="Online Ruler Guides: Calibration & Measuring Tips | A Online Ruler"
	description="Practical guides to calibrating your screen, reading ruler markings, measuring on phones and tablets, and understanding screen ruler accuracy."
	canonical="https://aonlineruler.com/blog"
>
	<div class="blog-shell">
		<a class="skip-link" href="#blog-main">Skip to blog guides</a>
		<header class="blog-header">
			<a class="blog-brand" href="/" aria-label="A Online Ruler home"><img src="/favicon.svg" alt="" aria-hidden="true" /><span>A Online Ruler</span></a>
			<nav aria-label="Main navigation"><a class="blog-home-link" href="/">Home <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a href="/ruler">Ruler</a><a href="/calibration">Calibration</a><a href="/about-us">About</a></nav>
			<a class="blog-header-cta" href="/ruler">Open the ruler <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
			<button class="blog-theme-toggle site-theme-switch" id="blog-theme-toggle" type="button" role="switch" aria-checked="false" aria-label="Switch to dark mode" title="Switch to dark mode"><span class="site-theme-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="site-theme-mark site-theme-sun">☀</span><span class="site-theme-mark site-theme-moon">☾</span><span class="site-theme-thumb"><span id="blog-theme-icon">☾</span></span></span></button>
		</header>
		<main id="blog-main">
			<div class="blog-top-controls"><LanguageSelector currentPath="/blog" /></div>
			<section class="blog-hero" aria-labelledby="blog-title">
				<div class="blog-kicker"><span></span> THE A ONLINE RULER BLOG</div>
				<h1 id="blog-title">Measure with a little more confidence.</h1>
				<p>Clear, practical notes on screen measurement, display calibration, and getting a useful reading from the ruler you already have open.</p>
				<a href="#guides" class="blog-hero-link">Browse the guides <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>
				<div class="blog-hero-art" aria-hidden="true"><div class="art-screen"><div class="art-screen-top"></div><div class="art-crosshair art-crosshair-x"></div><div class="art-crosshair art-crosshair-y"></div><span class="art-coordinate">X 4.2 cm&nbsp; · &nbsp;Y 2.8 cm</span></div><div class="art-ruler"><span>0</span><i></i><span>1</span><i></i><span>2</span><i></i><span>3</span><i></i><span>4</span></div><span class="art-glow"></span></div>
			</section>

			<section class="guide-index" id="guides" aria-labelledby="guides-title">
				<div class="guide-index-heading"><div><div class="blog-kicker">FIELD NOTES</div><h2 id="guides-title">Guides for everyday measuring</h2></div><span>01 — 04</span></div>
				<div class="guide-grid">
					{guides.map((guide) => <a class="guide-card" href={`#${guide.id}`}><div class="guide-card-top"><span>{guide.category}</span><span>{guide.number}</span></div><h3>{guide.title}</h3><p>{guide.summary}</p><div class="guide-card-bottom"><span>{guide.read}</span><span class="guide-arrow" aria-hidden="true">↗</span></div></a>)}
				</div>
			</section>

			<section class="featured-guide" aria-labelledby="calibrate-screen">
				<div class="featured-guide-label"><span class="featured-dot"></span> START HERE <span class="featured-rule"></span> {guides[0].read.toUpperCase()}</div>
				<article class="article-body">
					<div class="article-heading"><span class="article-number">01</span><div><div class="blog-kicker">CALIBRATION</div><h2 id="calibrate-screen">How to calibrate an online ruler for your screen</h2></div></div>
					<p class="article-lead">A screen does not have one universal physical size. Display density, scaling, and browser zoom all affect how large a ruler appears, so calibration gives the on-screen marks a more useful starting point.</p>
					<h3>Choose a starting point</h3>
					<p>Open the <a href="/calibration">calibration page</a> and choose a method. A device preset uses a model’s listed pixel density. If you do not know the model, enter the screen diagonal and aspect ratio for an estimate. Browser display information can help as a quick baseline, but it cannot reliably identify every panel’s physical density.</p>
					<h3>Check it against something you know</h3>
					<p>For a closer result, align the on-screen reference with a standard bank card or compare a measured length with a physical ruler. Adjust until the known object matches the displayed scale. Keep browser zoom steady after calibration; changing zoom changes the relationship between screen pixels and the ruler.</p>
					<div class="article-callout"><span aria-hidden="true">i</span><p>Recheck calibration if you move the browser to another display, change zoom, or switch devices. Use a physical ruler or caliper when a close tolerance matters.</p></div>
					<a class="article-action" href="/calibration">Calibrate your display <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
				</article>
			</section>

			<section class="article-list" aria-label="More measuring guides">
				<article class="article-body compact-article">
					<div class="article-heading"><span class="article-number">02</span><div><div class="blog-kicker">MEASUREMENT BASICS</div><h2 id="measure-cm-mm">Centimeters, millimeters, and inches: a quick guide</h2></div></div>
					<p class="article-lead">Choose the unit that matches the dimension you need to read. The ruler switches between centimeters, inches, and pixels from its unit control.</p>
					<h3>Read the small marks</h3><p>Each centimeter is divided into ten equal millimeter steps. If an object ends three small marks after 2 cm, its length is about 2.3 cm, or 23 mm. On an inch scale, use the numbered inch marks first, then count the smaller divisions between them.</p>
					<h3>Convert when you need to</h3><p>One inch equals 2.54 centimeters, and one centimeter equals 10 millimeters. For example, 5 cm is 50 mm and about 1.97 inches. When converting a measurement, keep the original unit nearby so it is clear which value came from the ruler.</p>
					<a class="article-action" href="/ruler">Try the ruler in another unit <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
				</article>
				<article class="article-body compact-article">
					<div class="article-heading"><span class="article-number">03</span><div><div class="blog-kicker">ON YOUR DEVICE</div><h2 id="phone-ruler">How to use a screen ruler on a phone or tablet</h2></div></div>
					<p class="article-lead">A phone can show a handy ruler for small objects, even though its screen only displays part of a long scale at one time.</p>
					<h3>Make room for the measurement</h3><p>Open the <a href="/ruler">ruler workspace</a>, select centimeters or inches, and place the object along the visible marks. Use the edge controls to put the scale where it is easiest to reach. On a smaller screen, scroll the ruler to bring another section into view.</p>
					<h3>Calibrate before relying on the reading</h3><p>Choose a matching device preset if one is available, or compare the scale with a known reference. Avoid changing browser zoom after calibration. Touch screens can be convenient for a quick check, but keep the object flat and aligned with zero for the clearest reading.</p>
					<a class="article-action" href="/ruler">Open the measuring workspace <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
				</article>
				<article class="article-body compact-article">
					<div class="article-heading"><span class="article-number">04</span><div><div class="blog-kicker">ACCURACY</div><h2 id="screen-accuracy">How accurate is an online ruler?</h2></div></div>
					<p class="article-lead">An online ruler can show a useful screen scale, but the result depends on how well that scale matches the physical display.</p>
					<h3>What changes the reading?</h3><p>Screen size and pixel density vary between devices. Operating system scaling, browser zoom, and calibration also affect the physical size of each displayed mark. A preset or diagonal-based estimate is a starting point; comparing it with a known physical reference usually improves it.</p>
					<h3>Use the right tool for the tolerance</h3><p>For crafts, classroom activities, and quick household checks, a carefully calibrated screen ruler can be convenient. For a precise fit, a regulated measurement, or safety-critical work, verify the result with a physical ruler, caliper, or other suitable instrument.</p>
					<a class="article-action" href="/calibration">Review calibration options <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
				</article>
			</section>
			<section class="blog-endcap"><div><div class="blog-kicker">READY WHEN YOU ARE</div><h2>Put the guide to work.</h2><p>Open the ruler, choose a unit, and calibrate the display when the measurement calls for it.</p></div><a class="blog-header-cta" href="/ruler">Open the ruler <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></section>
		</main>
		<footer class="blog-footer"><a class="blog-brand" href="/"><img src="/favicon.svg" alt="" aria-hidden="true" /><span>A Online Ruler</span></a><span>&copy; 2026</span><nav aria-label="Footer navigation"><a href="/ruler">Ruler</a><a href="/calibration">Calibration</a><a href="/about-us">About</a><a href="/privacy-policy">Privacy</a></nav></footer>
	</div>
</Layout>

<style>
